+## Architecture
+
+[![Swarm logical architecture and pending integrations](docs/diagrams/architecture-overview-en.svg)](docs/diagrams/architecture-overview-en.svg)
+
+The diagram presents shared responsibilities; Rust integration is in progress.
+Dashed links identify pending identity integration and future confinement.
+Arrows represent logical exchanges, not permissions already guaranteed.
+[Open the diagram at full size](docs/diagrams/architecture-overview-en.svg) · [Mermaid source](docs/diagrams/architecture-overview-en.mmd).
+
+- **The human** sets the objective, limits and decisions within their authority.
+- **The root supervisor** owns the overall objective. Scope owners guide their
+  part; workers produce deliverables and evidence.
+- **The deterministic engine** retains state, observes and routes incidents,
+  checks preconditions and applies authorized decisions. Business judgment and
+  completion belong to the supervisor, subject to required checks.
+- **The independent reviewer** examines the candidate in a separate session.
+  Its opinion alone proves neither test execution nor acceptance.
+- **Providers and tools** perform work according to their capabilities and
+  effective permissions. A text connection alone supplies neither editing tools
+  nor OS confinement.
+
+Supervision follows **observe → decide → verify → apply → observe the effect**.
+Duration or silence is an observation; a stop request does not prove process
+termination. Refusals, uncertain effects and recovery must remain visible.
+See the [supervision contract](docs/en/RUN-SUPERVISION.md) and
+[reports, evidence and decisions](docs/en/AGENT-COMMUNICATION.md).
+
+Two UX applications are planned over the shared engine and API: **Projects and
+development**, then **Business processes**, with separable builds and deployments.
+This is an [architecture target, FR](docs/PRODUCT-ARCHITECTURE.md); the billing
+bench is neither a complete business application nor authorization for real payments.
 
 ## Memory and identity
 
-An agentic workflow gains continuity when experience carries forward, and control
-when permissions are explicit. Swarm connects these dimensions to its
-**deterministic engine**: agents propose and perform work; the engine applies
-rules and retains decision history.
-
-[![Available workflow and memory, with identity integration in progress](docs/diagrams/memory-identity-en.svg)](docs/diagrams/memory-identity-en.svg)
-
-Solid links show paths available in the current sources. Dashed links show
-pending identity integration. Memory supplies context and grants no permissions.
-[Editable Mermaid source](docs/diagrams/memory-identity-en.mmd).
-
-**Project memory — available in the current sources.** An experience or procedure
-from verified lessons learned can be admitted, versioned and explicitly selected
-for a future task. The delivered context retains the lesson, its usage conditions
-and source references. Replacement and withdrawal preserve old versions and
-already delivered contexts. This makes verified knowledge reusable and explains
-why it was passed on, without granting the agent additional permissions.
-
-**Agent identity and authority — integration in progress.** The internal kernel
-checks the actor, its instance, the relevant plan, permissions and their
-delegation chain, including expiry and revocation. The goal is to reject
-impersonation and unauthorized agent creation, including creation by a legitimate
-agent. Integration with launchers, memory operations and protected human approval
-remains unfinished; these protections are not yet guaranteed across all public
-flows. Attested ownership and authorship, along with memory usage rights, are
-also part of that remaining work.
-
-**Value for workflow management:** carry learning across missions, make context
-handoffs reviewable and progressively govern who can act or delegate. Templates
-define the workflow; memory carries experience; identity and permissions delimit
-the intended actions. This approach serves development missions and the
-[business process target](docs/PRODUCT-ARCHITECTURE.md), where continuity,
-responsibilities and human approvals must remain explicit.
-
-For example, a verification procedure admitted after one mission can be selected
-for the next with its provenance. Its content does not grant an agent permission
-to create a worker or approve a business action.
-
-See [project memory](docs/en/PROJECT-MEMORY.md), the
-[identity contract and its limits](docs/en/AGENT-IDENTITY-CONTRACT.md) and the
-[memory integration plan](docs/plans/deepagents-integration/PLAN.md).
